  "prediction": "**Expanding Access to Medication-Assisted Treatment (MAT) for Opioid Use Disorders**\n\nThe Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) has implemented various efforts to expand access to Medication-Assisted Treatment (MAT) for opioid use disorders. MAT combines behavioral therapy with medications such as methadone, buprenorphine, and naltrexone to treat opioid addiction. The report highlights the importance of MAT in reducing opioid use disorders and overdose deaths.\n\n**Key Findings:**\n\n1. HHS has implemented five key efforts to expand access to MAT, including grant programs and waivers for practitioners to prescribe buprenorphine.\n2. The Targeted Capacity Expansion: Medication Assisted Treatment – Prescription Drug and Opioid Addiction (MAT-PDOA) grant program provides funding to states to increase capacity for MAT and recovery support services.\n3. The Substance Abuse Service Expansion Supplement to Health Centers grant program provides funding to health centers to improve and expand substance abuse services, including MAT.\n4. The State Targeted Response to the Opioid Crisis Grants (Opioid STR) program provides funding to states to increase access to treatment services for opioid use disorders, including MAT.\n5. HHS has also taken steps to prevent diversion of MAT medications, including requiring OTPs and practitioners to maintain diversion control plans.\n\n**Challenges and Recommendations:**\n\n1. HHS has not established performance measures with targets to evaluate the effectiveness of its efforts to expand access to MAT.\n2. The department has not established timeframes for the evaluation activities, increasing the risk that the evaluation will not be completed in a timely manner.\n3. The report recommends that HHS establish performance measures with targets related to expanding access to MAT and establish timeframes for the evaluation activities.\n\n**State and Private Sector Efforts:**\n\n1. State health officials are offering outreach and training to providers to help expand access to MAT.\n2. Private health insurers are expanding access to MAT through outreach, training, and modifying health benefit coverage.\n3. National associations are helping to expand access to MAT through outreach and training for their members.\n\nOverall, the report highlights the importance of expanding access to MAT for opioid use disorders and the need for HHS to establish performance measures and evaluation timeframes to ensure the effectiveness of its efforts.",
